    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> made Rasa, later called Lhasa, the capital of the Tibetan Empire in the 7th century and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> and Ramoche temples there in 641 to house two Buddha statues.</li><li>After the empire fell in the 9th century political power lay elsewhere, but Lhasa became the centre of the Gelugpa school in the 15th century and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>'s government from 1642.</li><li>The Qing entered the city in 1720, a British expedition occupied it in 1904, and after 1950 and the flight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> in 1959 it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> Autonomous Region in 1965.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Ancient Tibetan documents call the place Rasa, meaning &quot;goat's place&quot;, a herding site. It was renamed Lhasa, &quot;place of gods&quot;, when it became the capital and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> was completed to house a holy statue of the Buddha.</p></aside>
    </section>

