    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">637</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> moves the capital to Rasa and raises the first structures on the site of the Potala</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">641</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> and Ramoche temples are built to house two Buddha statues</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1642</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> moves the centre of his administration to Lhasa with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1645</span><span class="rf-text">Reconstruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> begins on Red Hill</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1720</span><span class="rf-text">The armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> enter Lhasa</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1904</span><span class="rf-text">A British expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband">Francis Younghusband</a> captures and briefly occupies the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1959</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> and his associates flee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> after a failed uprising</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1965</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> Autonomous Region is established and Lhasa becomes its capital</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jokhang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jokhang.jpg" alt="Jokhang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jokhang_Temple_in_Tibet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">onwardtibet.org, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lhasa lies in the middle reaches of the Lhasa River, also called the Kyi River or Kyi Chu, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-tsangpo">Yarlung Zangbo</a>, in the centre of the Tibetan Plateau at about 3,600 metres, with mountains rising to 5,500 metres around it. Ancient Tibetan documents and inscriptions call the place Rasa, "goat's place", a herding site. The name changed to Lhasa, "place of gods", when it was established as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> was completed to house a holy statue of the Buddha. The name Lhasa is first recorded, as the name of the area's temple of Jowo, in a treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Tibet of 822 CE.</p><p>By the mid-7th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> had founded the Tibetan Empire, rising to power in the valley of the Yarlung Tsangpo. After conquering the kingdom of Zhangzhung in the west he moved the capital from Chingwa Taktsé Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongye">Chongye</a> County to Rasa, where in 637 he raised the first structures on the site of the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> on Mount Marpori. He is said to have married Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhrikuti">Bhrikuti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> in 639 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-wencheng">Princess Wencheng</a> of the Tang court in 641, and Bhrikuti is said to have converted him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. In 641 he built the Jokhang and the Ramoche temple to house two Buddha statues, the Akshobhya Vajra and the Jowo Sakyamuni, which the princesses are said to have brought to his court. In a Tibetan tradition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a> magically pinned down an earth demoness and built the foundation of the Jokhang over her heart.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/potala-palace.jpg" alt="Potala Palace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Potala_Palace_HQ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lhasa Government, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the empire fragmented, the importance of Lhasa as a religious site grew over the centuries. By the 15th century the city had risen to prominence after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Je Tsongkhapa</a> and his disciples founded three large Gelugpa monasteries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-monastery">Ganden</a>, Sera and Drepung, as part of a puritanical Buddhist revival. The scholarly achievements and political skill of the Gelugpa lineage eventually brought Lhasa back to centre stage.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>, Lobsang Gyatso (1617 to 1682), moved the centre of his administration to Lhasa in 1642 and Lhasa then became both the religious and the political capital. Reconstruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> began in 1645 on Red Hill. The Potrang Karpo, the White Palace, was completed in 1648, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> used the Potala as a winter palace from then on. The Potrang Marpo, the Red Palace, was added between 1690 and 1694, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> was greatly expanded around the same time. Some wooden carvings and lintels of the Jokhang date to the 7th century, but the oldest of the city's extant buildings date to this second flowering. Norbulingka, the summer residence of the Dalai Lamas, was built from 1755.</p><p>By the end of the 17th century the Barkhor formed a busy market for foreign goods. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary Ippolito Desideri reported in 1716 that the city had a cosmopolitan community of Mongol, Chinese, Muscovite, Armenian, Kashmiri, Nepalese and northern Indian traders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> exported musk, gold, medicinal plants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and yak tails, in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, saffron, Persian turquoise, European amber and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coral.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama.jpg" alt="5th Dalai Lama" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thangka_of_the_5th_Dalai_Lama.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lhasa was the capital of the Tibetan Empire after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> moved it there. From the fall of the monarchy in the 9th century to the accession of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> the centre of political power in the Tibetan region was not in Lhasa. In 1642 the 5th Dalai Lama unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and moved his administration there with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> of the Khoshut, who remained a largely uninvolved overlord. Historians call the civil administration he and his intimates set up the Lhasa state, and its core leadership the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a>.</p><p>The armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> entered Lhasa in 1720, and the Qing government sent resident commissioners, the Ambans, to the city. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> suppressed a riot in 1750 he reorganised the Tibetan government and set up the governing council, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a>, in Lhasa in 1751. In January 1904 a British expedition captured and briefly occupied the city. Its leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband">Sir Francis Younghusband</a>, negotiated the Convention of Lhasa with remaining Tibetan officials after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a> had fled to the countryside. The treaty was later repudiated. All remaining Chinese troops left Lhasa after the Xinhai Lhasa turmoil of 1912.</p><p>On 2 November 1949 the Tibetan government wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> asking for talks; no consensus was reached. On 7 October 1950 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a> launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chamdo">Battle of Chamdo</a>, and in April 1951 a Tibetan delegation headed by Ngapo-Ngawang Jigme reached a consensus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on peace talks. In September 1965 the Tibet Autonomous Region was established with Lhasa as its capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lhasa was a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. In the first half of the 20th century nearly half its population were monks, though that figure may include monks from the surrounding monasteries who came for festivals. Pilgrims walked two sacred circuits: the Barkhor, about one kilometre long around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a>, and the Lingkhor, 8 kilometres long around old Lhasa, the Potala and Chokpori hill. The Barkhor had four large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> burners in the four directions, burning constantly to please the gods protecting the Jokhang. Chokpori held a famous medical school. Twenty-two parks, the lingkas, once surrounded the city, where people picnicked and watched open-air operas on festival days.</p><p>The city was not wholly Buddhist. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> has been present since the 11th century, and two Tibetan Muslim communities lived in Lhasa with their own homes, food, clothing, language, education and trade. Four mosques stand in and around it, the earliest, Khache Lingka, dating to 1650. Most of the Chinese population before 1950 were merchants and officials, and the people of the Lubu section were descendants of Chinese vegetable farmers who came in the 1840s to 1860s after a Chinese official was appointed Amban. Traders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and Ladakh also lived there.</p><p>Figures for the old city's size vary. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1854 gave 42,000, a figure said to have fallen greatly afterwards. The 1910 to 1911 Encyclopaedia Britannica put the population at about 30,000 including the lamas, and Hugh Richardson gave 25,000 to 30,000 for 1952, or 45,000 to 50,000 with the great monasteries on its outskirts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama.jpg" alt="14th Dalai Lama" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tenzin_Gyatso_-_Trento_2013_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Niccolò Caranti, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A Tibetan tradition holds that after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a>'s death in 649 Chinese troops invaded and captured Lhasa and burnt the Red Palace. Chinese and Tibetan scholars have noted that the event appears in neither the Chinese annals nor the Tibetan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>, and one scholar suggested it may derive from an interpolation. In the 9th century Lhasa suffered extensive damage under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langdarma">Langdarma</a>, when sacred sites were destroyed and desecrated and the empire fragmented.</p><p>On 11 November 1750 the murder of the regent by the Ambans set off a riot in the city that left more than a hundred people dead, among them the Ambans.  In 1904 the British expedition captured the city, and the Xinhai Lhasa turmoil of 1912 ended with the departure of the remaining Chinese troops.</p><p>In 1959, following a failed uprising, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> and his associates fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Many important sites were damaged or destroyed, mostly but not solely during the Cultural Revolution of the 1960s. The Ramoche temple was gutted and partly destroyed in that decade and its famous bronze statue disappeared; in 1983 its lower part was said to have been found in a Lhasa rubbish tip and its upper half in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. Of the 22 lingkas only three survive, as dormitory blocks, offices and army barracks were built over the rest. Most of the old streets and buildings of the Barkhor have been demolished and replaced, and some buildings there were damaged in the unrest of 2008. A crackdown on protests followed in 2008.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhol-pillar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhol-pillar.jpg" alt="Zhol Pillar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zhol-pillar">Zhol Pillar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Zhol_PIllar_in_1949.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugh Edward Richardson, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Lhasa remains the chief city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jokhang">Jokhang</a> is for most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> the most sacred and important temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and a key centre of pilgrimage for centuries; it is presently controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> was the chief residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> during the uprising of 1959 the government converted it into a museum, and it was restored between 1989 and 1994. The Potala, the Jokhang and Norbulingka are part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and the Potala was inscribed in 1994. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> has expressed concern about the deterioration of Lhasa's traditional cityscape.</p><p>The Lhasa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhol-pillar">Zhol Pillar</a> below the Potala dates to about 764 CE and carries what may be the oldest known example of Tibetan writing, with the account of a Tibetan general's campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, including the brief capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 763. A Monument to the Peaceful Liberation of Tibet was unveiled in Potala Square in May 2002 to mark the 51st anniversary of the Seventeen Point Agreement, with an inscription describing the development of Tibet over fifty years.</p><p>Lhasa is the capital of the Tibet Autonomous Region. The 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 429,104 Tibetans, 76.70 percent of the population, and 121,065 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, 21.64 percent. A 2011 book estimated that up to two thirds of the city's residents are non-Tibetan, while the government states that Chengguan District is 63 percent ethnic Tibetan.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Lhasa, on the Kyi River of the Tibetan Plateau, became the capital of the Tibetan Empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songtsen-gampo">Songtsen Gampo</a> in the 7th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> made it the capital of his government in 1642, and since 1965 it has been the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> Autonomous Region.</em></p>

